 "chapter": "7",
 "title": "Trigonometric Substitution",
 "short": "Trigonometric Substitution",
 "lead": "<span data-concept=\"trigonometric-substitution\">Trigonometric substitution</span> turns an integral containing $\\sqrt{a^{2}-x^{2}},$ $\\sqrt{a^{2}+x^{2}}$ or $\\sqrt{x^{2}-a^{2}}$ into a <span data-concept=\"trigonometric-integral\">trigonometric integral</span>. Here each radical gets its substitution, $x=a\\sin\\ktheta,$ $a\\tan\\ktheta$ or $a\\sec\\ktheta,$ and a <span data-concept=\"reference-triangle\">reference triangle</span> carries the answer back to $x.$",
 "objectives": [
  "Solve integration problems involving the square root of a sum or difference of two squares."
 ],
 "summary_html": "<ul><li>For <span data-concept=\"indefinite-integral\">integrals</span> involving $\\sqrt{a^{2}-x^{2}},$ use the substitution $x=a\\;\\sin \\ktheta$ and $dx=a\\;\\cos \\ktheta d\\theta .$</li><li>For <span data-concept=\"indefinite-integral\">integrals</span> involving $\\sqrt{a^{2}+x^{2}},$ use the substitution $x=a\\;\\tan \\ktheta$ and $dx=a\\;{\\sec }^{2}\\ktheta d\\theta .$</li><li>For <span data-concept=\"indefinite-integral\">integrals</span> involving $\\sqrt{x^{2}-a^{2}},$ substitute $x=a\\;\\sec \\ktheta$ and $dx=a\\;\\sec \\ktheta \\;\\tan \\ktheta d\\theta .$</li></ul>",
