    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "tangent",
   "prompt": "<p>State the method of integration you would use to evaluate the integral $\\int x\\sqrt{x^{2}+1}\\;dx .$ Why did you choose this method?</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "solution": "Use <em>u</em>-substitution with $u=x^{2}+1:$ its differential $du=2x\\;dx$ already stands in the integrand, so $\\int x\\sqrt{x^{2}+1}\\;dx=\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\sqrt{u}\\;du=\\frac{1}{3}{(x^{2}+1)}^{3/2}+C.$ A trigonometric substitution $x=\\tan \\theta$ also works but takes longer.",
    "generated_by": "ai"
   }
  },
